  "full_response": "# **Exhaustive Guide to Linux System Administration**\n\nThis guide covers essential topics for Linux system administration, including **users and groups, permissions, networking, storage, services, security, monitoring, backup, and troubleshooting**.\n\n---\n\n## **Table of Contents**\n1. [Users and Groups Management](#1-users-and-groups-management)\n2. [File Permissions and Ownership](#2-file-permissions-and-ownership)\n3. [Networking Configuration](#3-networking-configuration)\n4. [Storage Management](#4-storage-management)\n5. [Service Management](#5-service-management)\n6. [Security Hardening](#6-security-hardening)\n7. [System Monitoring](#7-system-monitoring)\n8. [Backup and Recovery](#8-backup-and-recovery)\n9. [Troubleshooting Common Issues](#9-troubleshooting-common-issues)\n\n---\n\n## **1. Users and Groups Management**\nLinux uses a multi-user model where users and groups control access to system resources.\n\n### **1.1 User Management Commands**\n| Command | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| `useradd [username]` | Create a new user |\n| `usermod [options] [username]` | Modify user properties |\n| `userdel [username]` | Delete a user |\n| `passwd [username]` | Change a user's password |\n| `id [username]` | Display user and group IDs |\n| `whoami` | Show current logged-in user |\n| `su [username]` | Switch to another user |\n| `sudo [command]` | Execute a command as root |\n\n### **1.2 Group Management Commands**\n| Command | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| `groupadd [groupname]` | Create a new group |\n| `groupmod [options] [groupname]` | Modify a group |\n| `groupdel [groupname]` | Delete a group |\n| `gpasswd [groupname]` | Set a group password |\n| `usermod -aG [groupname] [username]` | Add a user to a group |\n| `groups [username]` | List groups a user belongs to |\n\n### **1.3 Important User Files**\n- `/etc/passwd` – User account information\n- `/etc/shadow` – Encrypted passwords (requires root access)\n- `/etc/group` – Group information\n- `/etc/sudoers` – Sudo privileges (edit with `visudo`)\n\n---\n\n## **2",
